1
00:00:01,161 --> 00:00:06,520
Everyone on the team now has VS Code, GitHub Copilot, and the Claude command line.

2
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,500
Powerful tools.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,441
But not everyone wants to live in a terminal.

4
00:00:10,701 --> 00:00:11,405
Meet Lumen.

5
00:00:11,405 --> 00:00:17,293
The same AI agents and skills, behind a friendly chat window that runs on your own computer.

6
00:00:18,759 --> 00:00:20,299
Lumen greets you by name.

7
00:00:20,299 --> 00:00:29,660
Instead of agents and prompts, you pick an assistant, a specialist with its own skills, start from a quick start, or follow a guided delivery lifecycle.

8
00:00:29,925 --> 00:00:33,642
Marcus is a business analyst on the Contoso CRM project.

9
00:00:33,642 --> 00:00:38,885
He needs user stories by Friday, and he has never written a prompt in his life.

10
00:00:40,099 --> 00:00:43,726
So he picks the Requirements Coach, and clicks one of its suggestions.

11
00:00:46,239 --> 00:00:50,741
The answer streams in, like chatting with a colleague who has done this a hundred times.

12
00:00:54,582 --> 00:01:01,408
User stories, Given, When, Then acceptance criteria, and the open questions to take back to the business.

13
00:01:04,501 --> 00:01:06,414
It even suggests a next step.

14
00:01:06,414 --> 00:01:07,403
One click away.

15
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:15,689
Every reply can be retried, branched into a new chat, rated, or saved as a document.

16
00:01:15,948 --> 00:01:19,315
The business wants Word, so, Save as, Word document.

17
00:01:19,315 --> 00:01:23,329
Real headings, lists, and tables, saved to his project folder.

18
00:01:26,538 --> 00:01:29,320
Next, Marcus wants a helper for test scripts.

19
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:30,741
One sentence is enough.

20
00:01:36,453 --> 00:01:41,246
Lumen drafts the name, the icon, the instructions, and example requests.

21
00:01:41,246 --> 00:01:43,642
It even switches on the Excel skill.

22
00:01:50,046 --> 00:01:51,685
No configuration files.

23
00:01:51,685 --> 00:01:52,397
No jargon.

24
00:01:52,397 --> 00:01:53,822
Just a new teammate.

25
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,294
Now meet Priya, the project manager.

26
00:01:57,294 --> 00:02:04,430
Her world is a Microsoft Project plan and a stack of Excel workbooks, and the steering committee is on Thursday.

27
00:02:07,488 --> 00:02:09,643
One click sets up the project folder.

28
00:02:14,131 --> 00:02:24,499
Here is the plan from Microsoft Project, forty two tasks with a baseline, plus a budget tracker, a RAID log, timesheets, and this week's progress update in Excel.

29
00:02:30,015 --> 00:02:31,515
She asks for a health check.

30
00:02:31,515 --> 00:02:34,516
Lumen has already picked the right plan from her folder.

31
00:02:38,701 --> 00:02:41,478
The Schedule Analyst reads the actual plan.

32
00:02:41,478 --> 00:02:45,869
Critical path, slippage against baseline, and who is over allocated.

33
00:02:48,144 --> 00:02:49,401
Amber, trending red.

34
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:56,313
Go-live has slipped ten working days, there is no float left, and one integration developer is the bottleneck.

35
00:02:56,313 --> 00:02:59,706
It even warns that the progress data is two weeks old.

36
00:03:00,877 --> 00:03:03,700
Any table copies straight into Excel or Outlook.

37
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:07,640
And because she wants this check every week, she sets it to repeat.

38
00:03:12,453 --> 00:03:13,487
Mondays at nine.

39
00:03:13,487 --> 00:03:18,661
Lumen runs it while it is open, and leaves the result waiting as an unread chat.

40
00:03:26,910 --> 00:03:29,580
Friday's progress arrived as a spreadsheet.

41
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:33,118
Instead of retyping it into Project, Priya hands it over.

42
00:03:37,128 --> 00:03:44,232
Lumen reads the workbook, updates the plan, saves a new version without touching the original, and checks the schedule again.

43
00:03:48,259 --> 00:03:50,883
Every change is listed, before and after.

44
00:03:50,883 --> 00:03:59,331
Progress is up from forty seven to fifty three percent, and it warns that the E R P delay will push go-live back another three days.

45
00:04:02,229 --> 00:04:05,003
And See changes shows exactly what moved.

46
00:04:05,003 --> 00:04:07,371
Every task field, before and after.

47
00:04:10,305 --> 00:04:13,441
When she wants a file, she lets Lumen edit, with one click.

48
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:25,216
And gets a colour coded Excel tracker, with a Gantt chart, ready for the steering committee.

49
00:04:26,424 --> 00:04:31,154
Finally, meet Dana, the delivery lead on the Online Permit Renewal program.

50
00:04:31,154 --> 00:04:36,515
Requirements, design, and stories all have to line up, and the auditors will ask how.

51
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:49,121
The lifecycle sample sets up her program documents.

52
00:04:49,121 --> 00:04:56,283
A project definition and requirements in Word, a solution blueprint, a data model, and a traceability workbook.

53
00:05:01,937 --> 00:05:03,675
First, a traceability check.

54
00:05:03,675 --> 00:05:09,574
The Traceability Analyst reads every layer of the workbook, from business requirement to story.

55
00:05:15,516 --> 00:05:18,259
Coverage for every layer, and a red status.

56
00:05:18,259 --> 00:05:21,641
Two stories marked ready would build the wrong thing.

57
00:05:21,641 --> 00:05:26,363
One builds a withdrawn requirement, and one still uses the old photo rule.

58
00:05:26,363 --> 00:05:33,957
It also catches a link to a requirement that does not exist, a story that skips design, and conflicting sources on SMS.

59
00:05:33,957 --> 00:05:37,148
Every finding has its cell reference and an owner.

60
00:05:42,708 --> 00:05:44,459
Next, the guided lifecycle.

61
00:05:44,459 --> 00:05:52,372
Refine the requirements, then functional design, technical design, and stories, with an approval gate between every stage.

62
00:05:52,637 --> 00:05:58,120
She names the run, picks the requirements and the project definition, and starts the first stage.

63
00:06:05,356 --> 00:06:12,929
The Requirements Refiner works in its own chat, with the source documents already pinned, and saves its output to the project folder.

64
00:06:22,881 --> 00:06:29,691
Thirty eight requirements, each with acceptance criteria and a source, all marked draft until approved.

65
00:06:29,691 --> 00:06:37,494
It spots the SMS conflict, follows the project definition, and lists the questions to take back to the business owner.

66
00:06:42,323 --> 00:06:45,575
Before signing off, an advisory traceability check.

67
00:06:45,575 --> 00:06:48,253
It reports gaps, and never changes a file.

68
00:07:07,988 --> 00:07:08,893
Dana approves.

69
00:07:08,893 --> 00:07:13,936
Her name and the time are stamped on the document, and the next stage unlocks.

70
00:07:13,936 --> 00:07:18,655
Send a stage back later, and everything downstream is flagged for review.

71
00:07:22,491 --> 00:07:29,329
Every thumbs up and down lands in Insights, so the team can see which assistants and quick starts are earning their keep.

72
00:07:29,329 --> 00:07:31,024
It all stays on this computer.

73
00:07:34,929 --> 00:07:39,795
Under the hood, Lumen drives the Claude command line, and it is pluggable.

74
00:07:39,795 --> 00:07:42,097
GitHub Copilot is one setting away.

75
00:07:45,362 --> 00:07:50,866
And every assistant and skill exports straight into VS Code, for the day you are ready for more.

76
00:07:53,466 --> 00:07:53,846
Lumen.

77
00:07:53,846 --> 00:07:57,840
The power of AI agents, made friendly for everyone on the team.
